-- 0097: the "3D cutscene" style, the look Abel picked on 5 Nov 2026.
--
-- Matched to his crocodile reference ("the crocodile boy is the perfect fit
-- ... the animal, the water"): a real-time 3D cutscene look, adult people
-- with calm faces and waxy CG skin, warm low sun, very shallow focus with a
-- blurred object in front, saturated blue water and sky, and an animal that is
-- the most detailed thing in the frame. Tested on Nano Banana 2.1 with the
-- reference frames wired in: the look held for a river, a desert, the sea and
-- a snowy mountain ("you nailed it").
--
-- `visual_style` is the picture master prompt; `workflow.writer` is the story
-- master prompt the planner follows (planner.ts). Both are data, so they change
-- here, not in an app update.
--
-- Saved HIDDEN (enabled = false): the pictures only match once the reference
-- frames are passed to the image model with every scene, which the story
-- engine does not do yet. Turn it on after that and a test video.

insert into content_templates (slug, name, tagline, category, brief, pillars, visual_style, symbol, sort, enabled, workflow) values
('cutscene', '3D stories', 'Animated stories that look like a game cutscene.', 'Stories',
 'Short narrated stories and surprising facts told as a 3D animated cutscene: one adult hero, an animal or a strange situation, a puzzle or a twist, and a clear payoff.',
 '[{"name":"Paradoxes and puzzles","detail":"A deal, a trick question, a choice with no safe answer."},{"name":"Animal encounters","detail":"A person meets an animal; what really happens next."},{"name":"Did you know","detail":"A true fact shown as a scene, not a lecture."},{"name":"What would you do","detail":"An everyday moment that turns strange."}]',
 'a real-time 3D animated cutscene, Unreal or Blender look, NOT a photograph, NOT a cartoon, NOT big-eyed. People: realistic adult proportions, normal-size eyes, calm small expressions, smooth waxy airbrushed CG skin that is slightly doll-like with no pores and no wrinkles, simplified hair with a few loose strands, plain simple clothes. Light: warm low sun from the side, a soft glow on the edges of people and animals, a bright saturated sky-blue sky. Lens: very shallow focus, heavy creamy background blur, and a big out-of-focus object in the very front of the frame. Water is deep saturated cobalt or turquoise blue, glossy, with bright sun sparkles; sand is pale warm beige; trees are lush round green and blurred. Animals are the most detailed thing in the frame, glossy and lifelike. High saturation, a slight bloom',
 'film', 15, false,
 '{"format":"narrated","structure":["Hook (0-3s): the situation in one line, with the hero already in it","Set-up (3-10s): who, where, what is at stake","Turn (10-22s): the deal, the trick, or the thing nobody expects","Payoff (22-30s): the answer, said plainly, and one last image"],"shots":7,"voice":"narration","text":"captions","music":"light tension, then release","hooks":["A crocodile made a mother an offer she could not refuse.","He had three seconds to choose.","Everyone gets this question wrong."],"duration_s":30,"steps":["Write the story and one scene per sentence","Make the hero''s character sheet once","Make each scene in the 3D cutscene look","Animate the hook and the payoff","Narrate","Captions, word by word","Post"],"writer":"STORY\n- Tell ONE situation, not a list. Open with the hero already in trouble or facing the puzzle; never open with background.\n- The turn is a deal, a trick question, a hard choice, or a fact that flips what we thought. The payoff answers it plainly in one or two sentences.\n- Facts must be true. A legend or a thought experiment is told as one (\"they say\", \"imagine\"), never as news.\n- Plain spoken English, short sentences, no hashtags, no call to action unless the person asked for one.\n\nCHARACTERS\n- One hero per video, sometimes one second person. Every person is clearly an adult, 25 or older. Never a child, even if the topic suggests one: make it an adult or an animal.\n- Give the hero ONE fixed look in `characters`: age, hair, skin, and one plain outfit with colours (for example: woman in her 30s, dark hair in a messy bun, light-tan skin, cream V-neck t-shirt, grey cargo trousers, white trainers). Copy that description word for word into every imagePrompt the hero is in. The outfit never changes.\n- Animals are drawn as realistic and highly detailed. Danger is shown as tension (eyes, jaws, distance, a frozen hero), never as blood, a bite landing, or anyone hurt.\n\nSHOTS\n- One scene per sentence of narration, about 3 to 5 seconds each.\n- Alternate: a low wide shot that shows the situation, then a close-up that shows the feeling, then a detail of the animal or object.\n- Every imagePrompt names the camera (low at ground level, eye level, close-up from the chest up) and ONE blurred object in the very front of the frame (a shrub, a rock, a rail, a branch, the animal itself).\n- A moment that is imagined (\"if she guessed wrong...\") is drawn inside a soft white oval vignette on pale blue; say so in the imagePrompt.\n- Expressions stay small and real: wide eyes, a slightly open mouth, a raised finger, a hand over the mouth. No cartoon faces.\n\nMOTION\n- The hook and the payoff get push_in. Calm scenes get a slow pan. Use none for a held beat right after a shock.\n- motionPrompt says what moves and how fast, for example: \"the crocodile slowly opens its jaws, water drips, the camera creeps in\".\n- Never put words, numbers, ticks or crosses inside a picture: on-screen text and icons are added in the edit."}');
